# `!has_error` is `!` and the name `has_error`, not the operator `!has` (LANGUAGE.md section 1): DS0111 with a fix to `not(has_error)`.
mode: syntax
---- query
from 'events.csv' | where !has_error
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..37
  PIPELINE@0..36
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    LITERAL@5..17
      STRING@5..17 "'events.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
    PIPE@18..19 "|"
    WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
    WHERE_OP@20..36
      IDENT@20..25 "where"
      WHITESPACE@25..26 " "
      PREFIX_EXPR@26..36
        BANG@26..27 "!"
        NAME_REF@27..36
          IDENT@27..36 "has_error"
  WHITESPACE@36..37 "\n"

error[DS0111]: `!` is not an operator
  --> query:1:27
  |
1 | from 'events.csv' | where !has_error
  |                           ^ this is C's "not"
  |
  = help: negate it with `not(...)`: `not(has_error)`
  = note: `!has_error` is `!` followed by the name `has_error`; the negated operator `!has` goes between two values: `a !has b`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from 'events.csv' | where not(has_error)
which parses without errors
